---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
import Aff from '../components/Aff.astro';
import data from '../data/fee-caps.json';
const rows = data.reviewed;
const allStates = ["Alabama","Alaska","Arizona","Arkansas","California","Colorado","Connecticut","Delaware","District of Columbia","Florida","Georgia","Hawaii","Idaho","Illinois","Indiana","Iowa","Kansas","Kentucky","Louisiana","Maine","Maryland","Massachusetts","Michigan","Minnesota","Mississippi","Missouri","Montana","Nebraska","Nevada","New Hampshire","New Jersey","New Mexico","New York","North Carolina","North Dakota","Ohio","Oklahoma","Oregon","Pennsylvania","Rhode Island","South Carolina","South Dakota","Tennessee","Texas","Utah","Vermont","Virginia","Washington","West Virginia","Wisconsin","Wyoming"];
const reviewedNames = new Set(rows.map(r => r.state));
const notYet = allStates.filter(s => !reviewedNames.has(s));
const title = 'Rental Application Fee Caps by State (2026)';
const description = `What landlords may charge rental applicants in ${rows.length} states, quoted from each state's statute: dollar caps, actual-cost rules, outright bans, and refund duties. Every row links to the law.`;
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":data.checked};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated={data.checked} schema={schema}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="lede">What a landlord may charge someone who applies to rent, state by state, taken from the statute itself. We have reviewed {rows.length} states so far; the rest are listed at the bottom as <em>not yet reviewed</em>. That doesn't mean they have no limit. We just haven't checked them yet.</p>

  <div class="note"><strong>The short version.</strong> Vermont bars application fees for residential rentals <Src href="https://legislature.vermont.gov/statutes/section/09/137/04456a" />. Maine, Rhode Island and Connecticut bar application or processing fees but let a landlord charge for a screening report, at actual cost in Maine and Rhode Island and up to $50 plus inflation in Connecticut <Src href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/14/title14sec6030-H.html" label="ME" /><Src href="https://law.justia.com/codes/rhode-island/title-34/chapter-34-18/section-34-18-59/" label="RI" /><Src href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/2024/sup/chap_830.htm" label="CT" />. New York has the lowest dollar cap we found: actual cost or $20, whichever is less <Src href="https://law.justia.com/codes/new-york/rpp/article-7/238-a/" label="NY" />. Colorado, Oregon and Washington tie the fee to actual or customary cost and set no dollar figure.</div>
  <p>If the applicant already has a screening report, some states bar or waive the fee. See <a href="/reusable-tenant-screening-reports-by-state/">reusable tenant screening reports by state</a>.</p>
  <p>If you need to order a new screening report, published prices and who pays are on our <a href="/tenant-screening-report-comparison/">report comparison</a> and <a href="/tenant-screening-cost-calculator/">cost calculator</a>, including <Aff id="smartmove" href="https://smartmove.pxf.io/c/6593174/1515180/17713" text="TransUnion SmartMove" />.</p>

  <p>To check one state at a time, with the packages that fit under its cap, use the <a href="/tenant-screening-checker-by-state/">tenant screening checker by state</a>.</p>

  <h2>Quick table</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>What the law allows</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {rows.map(r => (
        <tr id={r.code.toLowerCase()}>
          <td><strong>{r.state}</strong></td>
          <td>{r.cap}</td>
          <td><a href={r.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r.cite}</a>{r.url2 && <> · <a href={r.url2} target="_blank" rel="noopener">also</a></>}<br/><span class="meta">{r.sourceType}</span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>State-by-state details</h2>
  {rows.map(r => (
    <section>
      <h3 id={`${r.code.toLowerCase()}-details`}>{r.state}: {r.cite}</h3>
      <p>{r.cap} <Src href={r.url} /></p>
      {r.capUnverified && <p class="meta">{r.capUnverified}</p>}
      {r.notes.length > 0 && <ul>{r.notes.map(n => <li>{n} <Src href={r.url} /></li>)}</ul>}
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Can cities and counties add their own rules?</h2>
  <p>This page covers state law only. Some cities and counties have their own screening rules. Wisconsin's state extension office, for example, tells renters to "check local laws" because municipal codes can add protections <Src href="https://winnebago.extension.wisc.edu/files/2024/03/Application-Fees.pdf" />. Local rules will get their own pages.</p>

  <p>Other move-in charges are covered separately: <a href="/holding-deposits-and-admin-fees-by-state/">holding deposits and admin fees</a> and <a href="/prepaid-rent-limits-by-state/">prepaid rent limits</a>.</p>

  <h2>Not yet reviewed ({notYet.length})</h2>
  <p>We haven't checked these states' statutes yet. We'll add them as we finish each one, with a source link like the rows above. Until then, don't read anything into a state's absence from the table.</p>
  <p class="meta">{notYet.join(' · ')}</p>
</Base>
